\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Differentiating the actual source logarithm}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{For every real polynomial #{V\geq-b}, #{b\geq0}, and #{\lambda\geq0},
the \vocabk{normalized source logarithm}{fdip-00GM}
#{W_\lambda(J)} is defined for every real #{J\in H^*}.
All mixed coupling and source derivatives exist, commute, and are
continuous for #{\lambda\geq0}, #{J\in H^*}, with right coupling
derivatives at zero. For integers #{r,k\geq0} and source
directions #{\ell_i\in H^*},
##{\partial_\lambda^rD_{\ell_1}\cdots D_{\ell_k}
 \mathscr Z_\lambda(J)
 =\int_H(-V)^r\prod_{i=1}^k\ell_i(\phi)
       \ue^{J(\phi)-\lambda V(\phi)}\,d\mu_Q(\phi).}
The source derivatives of #{W_\lambda} at zero are the joint
cumulants of the #{\ell_i} under #{\mu_\lambda}.}

\proof{
\p{For #{0\leq\lambda\leq\lambda_0} and #{\|J\|\leq R},
the absolute integrand is bounded by
##{\ue^{\lambda_0b}|V(\phi)|^r
       \prod_i|\ell_i(\phi)|\ue^{R\|\phi\|}.}
If #{H\ne0}, take #{q>0} with #{Q\geq q\bbI}. Multiplication by the
Gaussian density is controlled by
##{\ue^{-q\|\phi\|^2/2+R\|\phi\|}
 \leq \ue^{R^2/q}\ue^{-q\|\phi\|^2/4}.}
Every remaining factor is bounded by #{C(1+\|\phi\|^2)^k}
for some finite #{C} and integer #{k}. The polynomial moment
bound of \ref{fdip-002W} therefore makes this an integrable envelope.
The same reasoning covers any finite number of further derivatives.
Dominated convergence applied to difference quotients proves the
formula, continuity and interchange of differentiation. For #{H=0}
the integrals are evaluations and the claim is immediate.}

\p{The integrals are strictly positive. In fact the supporting-line
inequality used in \ref{fdip-00GN}, and #{\mathbb E_{\mu_Q}J(\phi)=0},
give the denominator bound
##{\mathscr Z_\lambda(J)\geq
  \ue^{-\lambda\mathbb E_{\mu_Q}V}
 \geq \ue^{-\lambda_0\max(0,\mathbb E_{\mu_Q}V)}>0.}
It also applies at #{J=0} to #{Z_\lambda}. Hence taking logarithms
and their finite derivatives is justified on every such compact
parameter set. The finite derivative identity for a logarithm gives
the partition formula in [joint cumulants](fdip-00GM), since the
normalized moment-generating function equals one at #{J=0}.}
}

\p{If #{j\in H} is the metric representative of #{J}, square
completion gives #{W_0(J)=h(j,Q^{-1}j)/2}. More generally, for
#{Y=\ell(\phi)}, let #{\mathbb E_{0,t\ell}} denote expectation
under the Gaussian tilted by #{\ue^{tY}} and then normalized. The
mixed-derivative formula yields
##{\left.\partial_\lambda W_\lambda(t\ell)\right|_{0+}
 =-\mathbb E_{0,t\ell}V+\mathbb E_{\mu_Q}V.}
If #{\ell=\ell_X\circ P}, the exact marginal formula in
\ref{fdip-00GI} computes the same source logarithm from the retained
law, including all its source derivatives.}

\p{The integration argument uses
\citet{Theorem 1.4.49, p. 111}{tao2011measure}; the connected
interpretation uses
\citet{v2, Theorem 3.11, p. 38}{etingof2024mathematical}.
Mathlib's \leanref{ProbabilityTheory.iteratedDeriv_mgf} supplies
the scalar-source identity for a measurable real random variable
whenever its parameter lies in the interior of its exponential
integrability set. Here that set is all of #{\mathbb R} for
#{Y=\ell(\phi)} under #{\mu_\lambda}, by the displayed domination.
Its \leanref{ProbabilityTheory.analyticAt_cgf} also gives real
analyticity in this scalar source. These declarations concern the
source variable; the mixed coupling derivatives above use the
separate domination proof, with a one-sided boundary at zero.}
